  'The congregation dates from 1791. It met in a meeting house of 1802 or 1803, moved to Science Street in 1816, and in 1834–36 built a large stone church with a six-column portico at the head of Front Street, which passing trains on the nearby railroad shook until it was unsafe. The present church on Milton Avenue, of grey Vermont marble, was begun early in 1896 and dedicated on December 20, 1896; Sunday-school rooms were added in the 1950s and a rear entrance wing in 1996.',
